 went out to the Annex Gallery, where he spent the afternoon sitting before the painting His Father's Name, trying to see why the colors resembled his memories of home- or if they were only the colors he remembered his father's joy by. Maybe the painting was just of the feeling of his father, not what his home had looked like. Maybe the objects around him—his chair, the wall, his shoelaces—reminded him of a feeling and he painted that feeling. Maybe home was just something you felt, even if you weren't there.
In the end Jonas decided that he liked Weekly's art best. Maybe it was because he could see the animals really moving, maybe it was because the cats reminded him of home, but it was a good feeling, and that was rare. So he spent his Sunday sketching more cats. Four of them this time.
The first was black-and-white striped, curled under a faded green armchair;
